Hi Subba,

You can't have on a single machine 2 AdvFS domains with the same name.
What you should do is manually add the second domain (the one you did not
boot from) to your system, with a different domain name, and then mount it.

Do the following:

1. go to /etc/fdmns directory (this directory holds all the information
regarding your AdvFS domains) and mkdir a new directory. Name that directory
with the second domain name (don't name it root_domain because you can't
have 2 domains with the same name. you can name it something like
temp_domain).

2. cd the new directory you just created (i.e: /etc/fdmns/temp_domain).

3. make a static link to the devices the domain sits on. for example, if the
second root_domain (not the one you booted to) sits on /dev/rz3c, you should
make a static link to him in that directory: ln -s /dev/rz3c ./. If that
domain sits on more than one partition, link ALL of them.

4. now, run the showfsets command (showfsets temp_domain) in order to see
the filesets on the new domain.If everything is ok, you can now mount the
directory.

After these steps you will have:

A:
root_domain is the domain
root        is the fileset

B:
temp_domain is the domain
root        is the fileset

Good Luck.

--

<< Ron >>

