In article <38F5D34B.9DA4BB81@spectrum.ic.gc.ca>, Steve Borutzki <borutzki@spectrum.ic.gc.ca> writes:
>
>I need to add more swap space to an Alpha 2100 running 4.0D. Is there
>reason why I can't just add a 2 Gb harddrive and put that in the fstab
>as swap2?

No particular reason.  When page/swap space is actually allocated
a round-robin method is used.  If one page/swap device is significantly
larger than others it will get more allocations and thus more I/O
when the system pages.  If you only need the space for additional
virtual memory this won't be a problem.  If you do page and concentrate
the space to a single large device, that device could become a bottleneck.

>i.e. does swap have to be on the same HD as the root filesystem, can't
>find anything in the docs. For that matter, can/should it be on the same
>controller as the root FS?

Some versions (or perhaps some consoles) have had trouble performing
crash dumps when the dump space wasn't on the same controller as the
root file system.  This may be documented in the Release Notes.  For
paging, you should spread the I/O over as many disks and adapters as
available.

>Thanks

You're welcome.
