bobbyit@my-deja.com writes:

>I am having this following funny behaviour.
>"vi" ing a already existing file, on an NFS file system just hangs. I
>verified if it writes temporary file to the /var/tmp..and it does. only
>way to come out of it to do a ctrl-C and "vi" opens up with an empty
>file.
>I can "vi" a new (non-existent) file with no problems.

vi locks files with flock(2).  To support cluster work, we discarded the
BSD code behind flock and changed it to do fcntl(2) Posix style
locking (which works with NFS).  Unfortunately, now a lot of workstations
are using NFS locking that never did before, and a lot of those get
shutdown over night or just plain disappear.  NFS locking leaves notes
behind of the sort "When you next reboot, tell node xxx about that".
If you reboot and xxx is down, rpc.statd tries forever usually suffering
a sizable timeout on each try.

Fix is a new rpc.statd that is a lot more sensible about nodes that
go away, I think it's in the next patch kit.  Support should be able
to get you one earlier.  In the meantime, do "ls /etc/sm.bak" and
if that lists hostnames, "rm /etc/sm.bak/*" and restart NFS or reboot.

>Everything was fine up until I applied the patch kit #1. Then I
>uninstalled it and still the same problem.

Even a reboot without installing anything would have done you in.
