In article
<Pine.A41.4.21.0004031654580.192132-100000@homer06.u.washington.edu>,
M. Cao <mcao@u.washington.edu> wrote:

>Can you explain the message below, and how to fix ?
>It display on the console. My system running Digital Unix 4.0D.
>
>Console log for ugly.statgen.washington.edu
>AdvFS I/O error:
>    Domain#Fileset: home2_domain#home2
>    Mounted on: /home2
>    Volume: /dev/rz20c
>    Tag: 0x000054c4.8001
>    Page: 38
>    Block: 29334608
>    Block count: 16
>    Type of operation: Read
>    Error: 5
>    To obtain the name of the file on which
>    the error occurred, type the command:
>    /sbin/advfs/tag2name /home2/.tags/21700

/usr/include/errno.h says that "Error: 5" is EIO "I/O Error".  Check
your error log to see if you are experiencing any hardware errors from
/dev/rz20c.  Block 29334608 is the 512 byte sector (block) on device
rz20c that AdvFS experienced problems with.

If you are seeing hardware errors, then that is where you need fix the
hardware.

Whether there are hardware errors or not, you should use the suggested
command:

        /sbin/advfs/tag2name /home2/.tags/21700

to find out what file is experiencing problems, and see if all the data
for that file is OK.  If you suspect there is a problem with the file,
then restore it from a backup.  The error occurred while attempting to
read page 38 of the file (8K per page).



