Take a look at the contents of /etc/fdmns/root_domain/ when you are
booted from A.
It should contain a symbolic link
rz0a -> /dev/rz0a

or something like that. (If you are using LSM for root, it will look
different.)
Now when you boot off B, if you are using the same numbering for SCSI
devices in the
kernels on each, then presumably ls /etc/fdmns/root_domain/ will show a
link to a different
raw device, say rz1a. 
Now (still booting off B)

cd /etc/fdmns
mkdir root_A_domain
cd root_A_domain
ln -s /dev/rz0a rz0a
cd /tmp
mkdir root_a
mount root_a_domain#root /tmp/root_a

and you can see the other domain. Domains are just names; the fileset
names live on the disk.
