In article <1742d8f6.8b3458d8@usw-ex0109-070.remarq.com>, chico <fpereiraNOfpSPAM@sellbytel.ch.invalid> writes:
>
>I have a question: How to create new raw devices?

Direct access (raw) to disk partitions is provided by reading
and writing the character special file for the desired device.
If the special files exist, you don't need to do anything
special to use the raw device.  Just make sure it doesn't
overlap a partition that somebody else is using.  The disklabel
command does provide an "fstype" that can be used to indicate
that a partition is in use, but not by any of the usual consumers;
"raw".

If you've added a device and don't have the special device files,
the answer depends on the version.  For all the V4 and later
versions, the special files will be created when the system
boots, if the driver recognizes the device.  If the driver doesn't
than you have a configuration problem and you'll need to provide
a whol lot more information about the device and subsystem.

For V4 and earlier you can also use MAKEDEV to create the special
files for many devices.  The specific command depends on the type
of device.

For V5 you should probably use the appropriate management command;
hwmgr, etc.

