Hi,

Here is a simple C program:

void f(void);
main()
{
	f();
	f();
	f();
	f();
	f();
}

void f(void)
{
	system("time abc");
}

'abc' is a big executable of size around 2MB.
The output of the program is:

real   1.7
user   1.6
sys    0.0

real   1.8
user   1.6
sys    0.0

real   1.7
user   1.6
sys    0.0

real   1.7
user   1.6
sys    0.0

real   1.7
user   1.6
sys    0.0

However, if I change f() to 

void f(void)
{
	system("(time abc) &");
}

The output is then :

real   8.6
user   1.7
sys    0.0

real   8.6
user   1.7
sys    0.0

real   8.6
user   1.7
sys    0.0

real   8.2
user   1.7
sys    0.0

real   8.5
user   1.7
sys    0.0

These tests are done on 4.0e DEC. Can anyone explain me the reason
why the real time is so high when I run the test in background? One
reason for this may be that the jobs in background may run at a low
priority. But the interesting thing is that the real time is very
low in 4.0d DEC almost about 6-8 times less. Is anything changed
from 4.0d to 4.0e? Any help would be highly appreciated.

Best Regards,
