Howdy Bob,
   I support dbx for Compaq, and I've got a few ideas.  The first thing that
occurs to me is that
SurfMeshTool.cpp is possibly a C file that's been run through the
preprocessor.  Or maybe fortran. Dbx has no special knowledge of
preprocessed files (which are normally hugely expanded from the original C
code) and would not necessarily be able to match the line number in the cpp
file with the
line in the original file.  So you would need to check the original source
to see where to put your
breakpoint.  If the spot you want to place the break point is in a section
of code #included from
another file, well, we have some stuff in the upcoming release to help take
care of that, but that particular portion isn't in dbx just yet (you might
be able to set the breakpoint, but the source
won't display yet for the included portion.)

Bottom line, dbx does not think there are any source lines to set a
breakpoint at that particular location.  If you dump out the executable with
"odump -Pv" or with stdump, you will see a number of fields associated with
each procedure (grouped under each file).  Two of these will show what dbx
inspects to determine which lines are in a file.  These are lnLow and
lnHigh, which are the low
and high line numbers for each procedure.  If the line you are trying to set
a breakpoint is not contained in the enclosed set formed by this pair, then
dbx will report, "beyond end of file".
It could be before lnLow also, as the error message is a bit generic.

If you must set a breakpoint where dbx does not think there are any lines,
you can always use
a "stopi at 0x..." giving an absolute address, if you have a good idea where
you want to stop.

Hope that helps,
PeterT

