Hi all

Has anyone out there been able to make Tru64 successfully talk to an HP Laserjet
IIISi (i.e print both text, man pages, AND postscript)? Any help gratefully
received received. Below is a summary of what we have tried, in case it is of
any help. 

Marchelm Bomers


Currently we have our printer on the network and have an HP9000/720 act as the
print server. With the software on the HP, this works wonderfully well.
Unfortunately the HP system is about to be decommissioned (it is rather ancient
and not Y2K compatible). But we would like to retain the printer as it's the
only local one that we have that prints duplex.

We have tried placing an HP Jetdirect EX Plus (J2591A) box on the back of the
printer. We found that we could happily print postscript files (as long as the
old HP system didn't switch it to PCL mode at any time!) by having the following
entries in printcap:
	:rm=laser3si:
	:rp=raw:
Postscript printing then also works by sending .ps files direct to the Jetdirect
port (=9100). Unfortunately setting up tcp printing as per the Compaq
documentation failed, thus:
	:ct=tcp:
	:lp=@laser3si/laser3si:
and (in /etc/services)
	laser3si        9100/tcp
Utilising the pcfof filter also failed miserably (the lack of a suitable config
file probably didn't help here, but a customised one also failed to achieve any
worhtwhile results - mostly the printer recived invalid commands). Note here
that wwpsof is not available on the system (would be any better?).

We then set up the printer as shared on my PC (NT 4). We now found that we could
print text ok, but postscript now does not print (or more technically we can
manage to get ONE .ps file to print after printing some text after which the
printer hung (printing some text reset it ok.

Additionally the man pages do not print properly either, only the text portion
printing correctly (it would appear that the Jetdirect box does not understand
the print codes or simply "refuses" to pass them on correctly).
