Mark Wiederspahn wrote:
> 
> very slow NFS v3 from SGI to Tru64 5.0 server
> 
> Digital alpha pc 164lx with de500 tulip NIC, connected to 3com
> 3900 switch connected to SGI Origin with gigabit enet interface...

to make a very long and painful story short, the problem seems
to have been some sort of PCI contention. This is a Microway screamer
board with an alpha pc 164 lx chipset. Not a pc 164lx itself.

The slow NFS only from SGI was a result of how SGI sync's
up their NFS v3 datastream. This slowness was not as noticeable
with Sun solaris 2.6/2.7. Also, the gigabit enet had nothing to
do with the problem, nor did the switch. Data read from the
Alpha ran at expected speeds.

The most immediate demonstration of the problem was to send a
binary ftp file from any system to /dev/null on the Alpha. This
gave about 8192 packets/sec, or about 11.5 Mbyte/sec. When writing
locally to a ufs, advfs, or raw partition on the alpha, this dropped
to less than 2 Mbytes/sec. Reading had no effect. This effect
was demonstrated with two different disk controllers, and the
second set of boards worked fine on an old Dec 600 5/333.

Moving the DE500 from the 64 bit pci bus nearest the cpu to the
32 bit pci bus farther from the cpu while leaving the disk controller
in the 64 bit slot permits 8 mbyte/sec nfs v3 reads and 6 mbyte/sec
nfs v3 writes, advfs, with tcp or udp protocol. Interestingly, the max
throughput of binary ftp to /dev/null dropped to 9.5 Mbyte/sec
from 11.5 with the DE500 in the new slot.

thanks to all who suggested fixes.
mw



