Hi Steve ! 

Steve Cousins wrote:
> 
> Currently if I start a process in the background from a terminal window,
> if I close that terminal window the process also dies. 

This is normal - when you close a window (years ago: when you switch off 
a terminal), the "hangup" signal is sent to all processes controlled 
from that window/terminal.


> This doesn't happen with other versions of UNIX that I have used ... 

Are you sure ?  IMHO, that is _really_standard_ unless you change the 
signal handling settings. 

> ... and I was wondering if there was a way to turn this "feature" off. 

Yes, explicitly ignore the SIGHUP: Either code that into your program, 
or use the "nohup" command. RTFM about the stdout/stderr redirection 
that implies. 

> 
> I am using OSF/1 4.0b. 

Beware of Y2K - officially, you need to upgrade to 4.0D (or higher). 


HTH, Joerg Bruehe 

